format session-log-v1
stamp 20260825.132121
editor Claude Code
model claude-opus-5
voice Kyri
host Vultr SEA NixOS VPS ai-jail pier (Daylight DC-1 hand) -- Eastern (EDT)
title A choir for the largest unheard family -- 239 Lotus witnesses heard, and the runner learns to say when the tree moved
prompt Autonomous loop -- roster cold first, Standfast on reds, next agent-doable lap Lindy-first crux-first, rota row N mod 5, condense ITINERARY and refill Now, send each finished increment
think Opened cold and read the ledger first. Every row in construction/REDS.md through %220 reads CLOSED, so no standfast stood and the line was clear for constructive work. Took the previous lap's recommend at its first item: a choir for ales, 239 unheard witnesses in one family, a fifth of the whole 1,176 the reach meter counts.
think Measured before designing. tools/al/ holds 240 files, 239 of them ales_*_witness.rish. lotus/ holds 240 .rye modules, and the two sets do not correspond one to one: parse_int and tally_copy are helpers proven through the rungs that compose them, and ales_carried_highpass_witness builds lotus/tone_carry.rye rather than a module of its own name. So the honest bijection binds witnesses to the roster, never modules to witnesses -- the same asymmetry tools/ca/caravan_suite_witness.rish already names in its header, and for the same reason.
think Timed one rung rather than estimating: rishi/bin/rishi run tools/al/ales_gate_witness.rish took 5.97s. Multiplied by 240 that reads roughly twenty-four minutes, and I let that number carry the tier decision. It was wrong by six times, and the whole of REDS %222 is that mistake -- the sample was the session's first Rye build and paid the Zig toolchain's cold start once. Three clean full sings measured 233s, 303s, and 233s.
think Corrected the tier's REASON rather than only its number, which is the part that matters. At four to five minutes this choir is CHEAPER than caravan_suite at 8m31s, and caravan_suite stands on the every-lap tier -- so cost alone would have allowed tier lap. It stays on cadence for a second reason I had to actually think about: the every-lap roster already measures 20m20s to 22m19s read twice a lap, and Season C's Lotus rungs are stable while Caravan is the arc actually moving. The every-lap budget goes to the family that changes, and the roster header now says to revisit a row's tier when its family reopens.
think Split the guard along its own cost seam. tools/al/ales_suite_witness.rish sings and takes tier cadence; tools/al/ales_roster_witness.rish asks and takes tier lap. The split follows from what each half catches. Singing proves rungs that changed still work. Asking catches a witness BORN unheard, which is a question about the lap that just happened and is worth answering the same day. Caravan reached the same factoring by a different road, folding its bijection into an every-lap meter.
think Made the scan stricter than its elder on one point. tools/fixtures/caravan_roster_bijection_scan.sh greps its whole roster file, which passes only because that header happens to name no caravan witness. Mine strips comment lines first -- REDS %218's rule one room over, a citation in a comment is a promise rather than a call -- because my own header names tools/al/ales_roster_witness.rish and ales_gate_witness, and a whole-file grep would have registered a file that did not exist yet and read it as a phantom.
think Proved that strictness from the welcome side too, which is the half that distinguishes it from a scan that simply reads less. A planted pen holds one witness, its roster registers exactly that one in code and names a second only in a comment, and the scan is asserted to answer registered=1 rather than merely to pass.
think Chose registration over exemption when the guard bit its own author. ales_roster_witness.rish is itself an ales_*_witness.rish, so its first run refused: 240 on disk against 239 registered, naming the new file unheard. Registering it keeps the bijection true with exactly one name left out, the choir's own, because a choir need not register itself. A second exemption would be the hiding place this guard exists to close.
think Then a red found me rather than the other way round. The cold roster was still running when I wrote the first new file, and I let it run. Reading ps to see why it looked slow showed sow_witness starting minutes after that edit landed, which means the tail of a sixty-guard pass was measuring a tree the head had never seen -- and witness_reach and standing_equipment, which read the very files being edited, were still ahead in the queue.
think The run then produced its own evidence, sharper than the reasoning. All sixty guards answered green and the shell died at the last line on `syntax error: unexpected end of file from until command on line 134`, because sh reads a script lazily from disk by byte offset and this round had rewritten tools/fixtures/standing_equipment_run.sh underneath it. The runner is part of the tree its own run measures, which I had not seen until the shell said so.
think Booked REDS %221 and built the loom rather than writing a reminder. The runner takes a twelve-character digest of git rev-parse HEAD plus git status --porcelain before the first guard and again after the last. porcelain covers staged, unstaged, and untracked alike, which is what makes an untracked file written mid-run visible to it.
think Chose the digest's timing deliberately: tree_close is taken after the last guard and BEFORE the runner writes its own run card, so the reading describes the tree the guards saw rather than the tree plus the runner's bookkeeping. The card is gitignored on this pier so it makes no difference here, and a clone where it is not yet ignored still reads honestly.
think Chose to refuse rather than merely report, which is the opposite of the call the tier round made about staged_uncommitted, and the difference is worth naming. A staged file is ordinary work and a reading is right for it. A tree that moved mid-run means the verdicts describe no single tree, so the run has answered nothing -- and every guard line still prints above the refusal, so nothing it did learn is thrown away. A guard red stays the louder finding and keeps the verdict.
think Rota row 1 by lap index: git rev-list --count HEAD reads 3431, and 3431 mod 5 = 1, so Aether and Jupiter, why the work exists -- the compass, the Lindy effect, the grain and the crossing. The grain's own row 59 strand governed this lap directly: a guard that cannot red guards nothing. Both new guards are proven from the refusing side before either was called green.
obs The measurement that decides the tier. One ales witness 5.97s; the choir 240 rungs; the every-lap roster 20m20s to 22m19s, read twice a lap. Reach before 1,690 / 167 standing / 82 cadence / 514 sung / 1,176 unheard; after 1,692 / 168 / 322 / 755 / 937, ceiling lowered to 937 with no slack. Roster 63 rows: 61 lap, 2 cadence.
obs The cadence column rose by 240 while unheard fell by 239, and the one-apart is the choir itself: ales_suite_witness.rish is a new witness on disk that nothing counted before, so it joins total and cadence together without ever having been unheard.
obs The cold pass is reported as what it honestly is. Sixty guards, sixty green, and twelve of them answered before this round wrote its first file. The authoritative reading is the full hot pass after git add, which is what REDS %174 asks for anyway.
obs Every ales witness builds what it runs -- checked by reading all 239 rather than assumed, since caravan's REDS %92 was thirty-six rungs sharing a binary none of them built. So clearing lotus/bin before every sing costs nothing and makes cold-start self-sufficiency a fact the choir proves.
obs The second timing run returned 28s and a per-rung RED at ales_compress_witness, and the module was sound. Two sings at once delete each other's binaries, because the choir clears lotus/bin and 240 witnesses build into it. A false RED costs more than a slow guard, since Standfast stops the line for it and the line was never broken. tools/fixtures/ales_sing_lock.sh holds one sing at a time -- sow_project.sh's proven mkdir shape (REDS %193) in its own file rather than copied (REDS %215), a refusal rather than a wait, a killed sing's lock cleared by dead pid. Twelve behaviors proven both ways, the held case planted against a genuinely live process because two shell invocations in a row leave a dead pid and the second correctly takes the lock.
obs The same class fired once more before the round closed, and the choir caught it. The lock control's last check read the living lock as ABSENT rather than UNCHANGED, so it went RED inside a perfectly healthy sing -- the choir sings ales_roster_witness, which runs that control, and a sing holds the lock. It records the before-state at the top and compares at the bottom now. A guard refusing for a reason unrelated to what it guards is exactly what this round is about, and I wrote one while fixing one.
obs Folded the REDS headline's fold recital -- 5,999 bytes on one line, longer than the rest of the page's prose -- to construction/archive/REDS-fold-recital.md with its link depth moved, since reds_ledger_headline_scan.sh reads only the leading Rows: N count and reds_ledger_monotone reads the spine off disk. The pin went 24,512 to 19,810, which is what made room for %222 without a third fold. The card had named this door for two laps.
obs A third instance of the same class, caught by re-reading my own draft. The design note's opening put 1,690 tracked witnesses beside 56 sung every lap beside 1,177 unheard -- three numbers from three different moments, since seating the Caravan choir moved standing 56 to 167 in the middle of that same lap. Reading any two side by side without saying so is exactly what %222 is about. Corrected to name each moment, and the family table now says which reading its counts were taken against.
obs Stopped the hot roster at guard one to make that correction rather than editing across it, which is %221's own rule applied by hand on the lap it was seated. The pass restarted clean afterward.
obs Graded every artifact. The design note read A at 91 with a 35% negative share against Field's 30%, so it took a Gauge sweep -- five sentences turned affirmative, every claim held exactly -- and reads A at 95 with 22%. ITINERARY A at 95 and REDS A+ at 100, both at Meter. Nothing below B, so no molt frame was pushed.
obs Recorded checkpoint c2ea22613d before condensing the tier round's account off the operator card. The card stood at 24,558 against the 24,576 it declares, so the new round had no room beside it; it reads 24,505 now with the seed-cadence paragraph shortened to its four gate readings and one prior-lap row shelved.
obs Folded REDS %220 to construction/archive/REDS-a-close-that-costs-twenty-five-minutes-rows-220.md with its link depth moved -- text and target both -- so %221 had room. The living pin read 24,943 with the row as first written and took two trims to reach 24,512. The headline fold recital is now a 6,000-byte sentence and wants folding the way its own rows do; named on the card as a door.
loom ales_suite passes=3 wall_s=233,303,233 rungs=240 conditions=warm_zig_cache_run_alone
